meta {
  name: An allocated employee never ranks
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/requisitions/{{jobId}}/candidates/{{allocatedEmployeeId}}
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("scored on request, Arjun is a strong fit", function() {
    const m = res.getBody();
    expect(m.name).to.equal("Arjun Nair");
    expect(m.score).to.equal(86);
    expect(m.verdict).to.equal("strong");
    expect(m.blockers).to.eql([]);
  });

  test("yet he is absent from the ranked candidate list", function() {
    // captured by 'Ranked candidates', which runs first in this folder
    const ranked = bru.getVar("rankedIds").split(",");
    expect(ranked).to.not.include("E1007");
    expect(ranked).to.include("E1005");   // scores 50, and still outranks him by being free
  });
}

docs {
  Arjun Nair scores **86 and `strong`** against J2001 — better than everyone except Priya.
  He is also `allocated`, and `store.rank()` only ever scores the bench, so he never appears
  in *Ranked candidates* no matter how good he looks.

  Two endpoints, two different questions:

  - `/candidates` — *who could we put forward?* Filtered by availability.
  - `/candidates/{employee_id}` — *how would this specific person score?* No filter at all.

  Collapsing them into one endpoint is how a system ends up either hiding a strong internal
  candidate or offering someone who is already on a project.
}
